From ed124dd46a2902d7d968e5f2c6c365407f31b9da Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jared Wyles <jared.wyles@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 20:04:35 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 37/39] ggml-metal: check every row-list entry before reading a
 block from the cache

ggml_flash_attn_ext_rows accepts any row list. Two Metal paths decided from a
block's first and last entries alone that the block was one consecutive
range: the host check that compiles the gather-free variant, and the
per-block shortcut in the vec kernel. A valid block such as [0, 99, 2, ...,
31] passed both, and the kernel read row 1 instead of row 99. The host
check also passed any block that started with padding without looking at
the rest of it, though the gather-free kernel skips such a block whole.

The host now checks every entry: a run of consecutive cells from the
block's first entry, then only padding, with a run ending inside its block
still read inside the cache. Every attention layer of a graph reads the same
lists, so each list is checked once per command buffer. In the kernel each
lane checks its entries of the block and the simdgroup votes.

test-backend-ops gains the ends_only layout: consecutive blocks between
blocks whose ends are a block apart but whose interior holds a row from
elsewhere in the cache. It fails on the general kernel's old shortcut as
well as on the gather-free variant.
